@h1 A thesis to a print-ready PDF h1@

Turn one @code .wit code@ file into a print-ready PDF essay — title, byline,
epigraph, numbered sections, a data table, a captioned figure, and a Works Cited
list — with no LaTeX preamble and no toolchain to set up. It is the whole
academic spine in plain text, and every piece of it is core vocabulary, so
nothing here needs a definition.

@h2 Step 1 — Title and byline h2@

Give the paper an @strong h1 strong@ title, then a byline whose lines are joined
by the void @code br code@ node so they stack inside one paragraph.

@@@pre
@h1 Let Them Sleep: The Case for Later School Start Times h1@

Jordan Ellis @br br@
Ms. Rivera — English 11 @br br@
5 July 2026
pre@@@

The three byline lines collapse into a single
@@@code <p>Jordan Ellis <br>Ms. Rivera …</p> code@@@ — consecutive non-blank
lines are one paragraph, and @@@code @br br@ code@@@ forces the visible break.

@h2 Step 2 — Epigraph h2@

A @strong blockquote strong@ holds the quotation and its attribution.

@@@pre
@blockquote "Sleep is the golden chain that ties health and our bodies together." — Thomas Dekker blockquote@
pre@@@

@h2 Step 3 — Thesis and sections h2@

Body text is just prose — no indentation, a blank line between paragraphs. Open
each section with an @strong h2 strong@ and mark emphasis with @code _italics_ code@
or @code *bold* code@ (or the @strong em strong@ and @strong strong strong@ nodes).

@@@pre
@h2 The Teenage Clock Runs Late h2@

The heart of the problem is not attitude but chemistry — teenagers lose an hour
or more of the rest their developing brain requires @em every single night em@.
pre@@@

@h2 Step 4 — A data table h2@

Pass the rows inline. The @strong first strong@ inner array is the header row and
becomes @code th code@ cells; every later array is a @code tr code@ of @code td code@.

@@@pre
@table |caption Recommended versus actual sleep for adolescents| |rows [[Group, Recommended sleep, Typical school night], [Teens 13 to 18, 8 to 10 hours, 6.5 to 7 hours]]|
pre@@@

Cells are comma-separated @em within a row array em@ — that is safe. The comma
restriction is on record fields inside @@@code { } code@@@, not on table rows.

@h2 Step 5 — A captioned figure h2@

@strong figure strong@ wraps an @strong img strong@ and a @strong figcaption strong@.
Figures centre by default; @code size code@ is a preset — @code small code@ (220px),
@code medium code@ (380px), @code large code@ (600px), @code full code@ (100%) — or a
number or percentage.

@@@pre
@figure
  @img |src assets/attendance.svg| |alt Bar chart: attendance rising from 82 to 94 percent after a later start| |size medium|
  @figcaption Figure 1. On-time attendance before and after a district shifted its start from 7:30 to 8:35. figcaption@
figure@
pre@@@

The @code src code@ path is relative to the @code .wit code@ file, and
@code alt code@ text is required for accessibility.

@h2 Step 6 — Works Cited h2@

An ordered list of entries; italicise source titles with @strong em strong@.

@@@pre
@h2 Works Cited h2@

@ol
  @li American Academy of Pediatrics. "School Start Times for Adolescents." @em Pediatrics em@, vol. 134, no. 3, 2014, pp. 642–649. li@
  @li Dekker, Thomas. @em The Pleasant Comedy of Patient Grissell em@. 1603. li@
ol@
pre@@@

@h2 Step 7 — Render to PDF h2@

@@@pre
wit build essay.wit -o essay.pdf
pre@@@

@strong Result. strong@ A three-page PDF (verified at 158,820 bytes from the
worked example) with the title, byline, epigraph, five sections, the sleep
table, the attendance figure, and the four-entry Works Cited list. PDF output
paginates the default HTML theme through a headless system Chrome — swap
@code -o essay.html code@ for a styled web page, or add @code --fragment code@ to
inspect the raw markup.

@h2 Pitfalls h2@

@ul
@li @strong Use real headings. strong@ Don't reach for the older thesis example's
@code ## code@-style templates — those emit literal text in HTML, not headings.
Write @@@code @h2 … h2@ code@@@ directly, as this recipe does. li@
@li @strong Nest emphasis outer-italic. strong@ @@@code _*x*_ code@@@ leaves the
inner @code * code@ literal; write @@@code *_x_* code@@@ instead. li@
@li @strong No commas inside a record. strong@ A value like
@code Attention, Perception code@ inside @@@code { } code@@@ splits into two
fields. Inline table rows are fine — the comma there is a cell separator. li@
ul@

@h2 Variations h2@

@ul
@li @strong In-text citations. strong@ Define a short cite template and named-idea
definitions once, then cite by idea in prose — see
@a |href /docs/recipes/bibliography/| A bibliography across files a@. li@
@li @strong Web instead of print. strong@ @code -o essay.html code@ gives the
styled document; @code --raw code@ gives a reset-only page you style yourself. li@
ul@

@h2 See also h2@

@a |href /docs/recipes/annotated-essay/| The annotated essay a@ walks this exact
file line by line;
@a |href /docs/recipes/creative-writing/| Creative writing a@ covers flat prose;
the @a |href /docs/reference/core-vocabulary/| core vocabulary reference a@ lists
every built-in node used here.
